.\" ABSD manual page source (mdoc(7)); rendered by scripts/render-man.sh.
.Dd November 5, 2026
.Dt PS 1
.Os ABSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm ps
.Nd inspect session processes or current kernel process state
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl A
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fl A
runs the shipped
.Pa /bin/ps
program and reports the kernel's boot-scoped process identities, parent,
running/runnable/waiting/terminal state, wait class, handle count and CPU
claim. It works in pipelines and does not acquire state through SSH or FSD.
Each row is a separate snapshot. Image indices identify the boot worker,
file server or a filesystem-loaded image; command arguments, CPU percentages
and per-process memory are not reported. No process-control right is granted.
.Pp
The bare command
.Nm
without arguments is a builtin of
.Xr absd-sh 1 .
The shell asks its session service on the control channel, and the service
answers for that session.
This builtin view is limited to the current session; use
.Fl A
for kernel process state across the system.
Invoking
.Pa /bin/ps
directly, with no arguments or with
.Fl A ,
always selects the kernel view instead.
.Pp
The session builtin's answer has three parts:
.Bl -enum
.It
The session: its service-assigned number, whether it
has a terminal, and how long ago it connected.
.It
The processes the server can name: those it holds a process handle to.
Normally this is the session's shell alone. The shell owns its command
processes; their handles are not held by the session service, so the
builtin does not name them. Use the kernel view to inspect those rows.
.It
What the server can only count:
.Ql other holders: input N, file server channels M .
.Ar N
is how many more read ends of the session's input pipe exist than the
shell's own, and
.Ar M
how many more write ends of the session's file server channels than the
server's and the shell's.
These are counts of additional handle holders, not a process census.
They can include a running command or an adopted descendant; a nonzero
count does not by itself identify that process or grant control over it.
.El
.Pp
.Nm
may start a pipeline
.Pq Ql ps | wc -l
and may be redirected.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
The session builtin returns 0 when its service answered, or 1 when its
session control channel is absent or the service did not answer.
The kernel-view program returns 0 after reading the process snapshots,
or 1 when a snapshot is unavailable.
Unsupported arguments return 2; the supported kernel-view option is
.Fl A .
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
absd$ ps
session 3 (this session), terminal, connected 41 s
  PID PROGRAM
   23 /bin/absd-sh
other holders: input 0, file server channels 0
absd$ ps -A
  PID  PPID STATE     WAIT       HANDLES CPU IMAGE
\&...
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr absd-sh 1 ,
.Xr process 7
.Sh CAVEATS
The counts are of handles, not processes: one leftover process that holds
both the input and a channel adds one to each.
They are a snapshot; a leftover process may end at any moment.
.Pp
The process table bound is queried from the kernel. If that query is unsupported
or malformed, ps exits with status 1 and reports that no partial census was
produced. An older ps that assumes sixteen slots can miss upper-half processes
on the 32-slot profile. Snapshots remain independent per row.
